{"title":"BITSAT","category":"Engineering","bank":"bitsat","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","count":16,"questions":[{"id":17690,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"In reaction $\\mathrm{BF}_3+3 \\mathrm{LiBH}_4 \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{LiF}+X ; X$ is","options":["$\\mathrm{B}_4 \\mathrm{H}_{10}$","$\\mathrm{B}_2 \\mathrm{H}_6$","$\\mathrm{BH}_3$","$\\mathrm{B}_3 \\mathrm{H}_8$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$2 \\mathrm{BF}_3+3 \\mathrm{LiBH}_4 \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{~B}_2 \\mathrm{H}_6+3 \\mathrm{LiF}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2023 (Memory Based Paper 2)","_marksId":"65b7b87e97ae24e9e2db8ce3"},{"id":17691,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"What is $x$ in the following reaction?<br />$\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{s})+\\mathrm{NaOH}(\\mathrm{aq})+\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) \\rightarrow x+\\mathrm{H}_2(\\mathrm{~g})$","options":["$\\mathrm{Na}_2\\left[\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{OH})_4\\right]^{-}$","$\\mathrm{Na}^{+}\\left[\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{OH})_4\\right]^{-}$","$\\mathrm{Na}_2\\left[\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{OH})_6\\right]^{-}$","$\\mathrm{Na}^{+}\\left[\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{OH})_6\\right]^{-}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$2 \\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{s})+2 \\mathrm{NaOH}(\\mathrm{aq})+6 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) \\rightarrow2 \\mathrm{Na}^{+}\\left[\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{OH})_4\\right]^{-}(\\mathrm{aq})+3 \\mathrm{H}_2(\\mathrm{~g})$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2023 (Memory Based Paper 1)","_marksId":"65b7b87497ae24e9e2db8c7e"},{"id":17692,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Ge (II) compounds are powerful reducing agents whereas $\\mathrm{Pb}$ (IV) compounds are strong oxidants. It can be because","options":["$\\mathrm{Pb}$ is more electropositive than $\\mathrm{Ge}$.","ionisation potential of lead is less than that of Ge.","ionic radii of $\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Pb}^{4+}$ are larger than that of $\\mathrm{Ge}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Ge}^{4+}$","more pronounced inert pair effect in lead has."],"answer":3,"solution":"Inert pair effect is more pronounced in heavier members like Pb.\r\nHence, $\\mathrm{Pb}$ (IV) compounds act as strong oxidising agents and are reduced to more stable $\\mathrm{Pb}$ (II) compounds.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2022","_marksId":"64c0e8316f5bfc7ab78aeb63"},{"id":17693,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"The number of $3 \\mathrm{C}-2 e^{-}$bonds present in diborane is","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"solution":"The structure of diborane is\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252F-4EAmcvZnHO8X3GVHSqcsEvyeQS8-dTaftJ_-g9aGeo.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F-4EAmcvZnHO8X3GVHSqcsEvyeQS8-dTaftJ_-g9aGeo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F-4EAmcvZnHO8X3GVHSqcsEvyeQS8-dTaftJ_-g9aGeo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\r\nIn this structure, there are two $3 \\mathrm{C}-2 e^{-}$bonds. 3 atoms, $\\quad \\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{B}$ share two electrons and form an angular geometry, leading bent bond, also known as banana bond.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2022","_marksId":"64c0e8316f5bfc7ab78aeb67"},{"id":17694,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Diborane $\\left(\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}\\right)$ reacts independently with $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ to produce, respectively:","options":["$\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{BO}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{BH}_{4}\\right]^{-}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{HBO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{BO}_{3}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{BO}_{3}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2021","_marksId":"64c0e8266f5bfc7ab78aeb07"},{"id":17695,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Identify the incorrect statement:","options":["In $\\left(\\mathrm{Si}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{9}\\right)^{6-},$ tetrahedral $\\mathrm{SiO}_{4}$ units share two oxygen atoms.","Trialkylchlorosilane on hydrolysis gives $\\mathrm{R}_{3} \\mathrm{SiOH}$","$\\mathrm{SiCl}_{4}$ undergoes hydrolysis to give $\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{SiO}_{4}$.","$\\left(\\mathrm{Si}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{9}\\right)^{6-}$ has cyclic structure."],"answer":1,"solution":"The hydrolysis of Trialkylchlorosilane $\\mathrm{R}_{3} \\mathrm{SiCl}$ yields dimer:\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252FE2EfFeWjsUrwDgG-xoAtPamBEslUShffPJuvUT32pto.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FE2EfFeWjsUrwDgG-xoAtPamBEslUShffPJuvUT32pto.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FE2EfFeWjsUrwDgG-xoAtPamBEslUShffPJuvUT32pto.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2020","_marksId":"64c0e8166f5bfc7ab78aea78"},{"id":17696,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Among the following substituted silanes, the one which will give rise to cross linked silicone polymer on hydrolysis is?","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>Si</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>SiCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><msub><mi>SiCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>SiCl</mi></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>Among the following substituted silanes, the one which will give rise to cross-linked silicone polymer on hydrolysis is trichloroalkylsilane <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>RSiCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>RSCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> gives cross-linked silicon polymer on hydrolysis.</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252F3339b557-90b1-403d-af07-adf5f3a28ca7-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F3339b557-90b1-403d-af07-adf5f3a28ca7-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F3339b557-90b1-403d-af07-adf5f3a28ca7-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 600px; height: 315px;\" /></p><p> </p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2019","_marksId":"64c0e8076f5bfc7ab78ae9f1"},{"id":17697,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"The order of acidic strength of boron trihalides","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>The strength of acidic character of boron trihalides depends upon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pπ</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>pπ</mi></math> back bonding.</p><p>In boron trihalides, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pπ</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>pπ</mi></math> -back bonding occurs due to empty orbital of boron and filled orbitals of halogens.</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252Fbf2f12fc-7efd-4bd8-b002-c137c0c9320c-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fbf2f12fc-7efd-4bd8-b002-c137c0c9320c-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fbf2f12fc-7efd-4bd8-b002-c137c0c9320c-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 398px; height: 255px;\" /></p><p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pπ</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>pπ</mi></math> back bonding is shown maximum by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, as the size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> are small and comparatively same. Due to this effect tendency of accepting lone-pair of electrons of boron decreases as size of halogen increases. The order of size of halogens are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo><</mo><mi>Cl</mi><mo><</mo><mi>Br</mi><mo><</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>. Thus, acidic nature is in order</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>BI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2019","_marksId":"64c0e8076f5bfc7ab78ae9f4"},{"id":17698,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"A mixture of Boron trichloride and hydrogen is subjected to silent electric discharge to form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCl</mi></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math> is mixed with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and heated to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>200</mn><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> to form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></math>. The formula of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></math> is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>BO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>When a mixture of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is subjected to silent electric discharge, diborane and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCl</mi></math> are formed. Diborane reacts with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> to give Borazine (inorganic benzene).</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><munderover><mo>→</mo><mi>discharge</mi><mi>Electric</mi></munderover><munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mfenced></munder><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>HCl</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><mrow><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></munder><mover><mo>⟶</mo><mrow><mn>200</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mover><munder><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>Borazine</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2018","_marksId":"64c0e7fa6f5bfc7ab78ae985"},{"id":17699,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"The correct statement about silicone is","options":["They are ketones with silyl group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>SiH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced></math> similar to alkyl, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>SiH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>CO</mi></math>","They are synthetic polymer containing repeated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SiO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>units</mi></math>.","They are formed by hydrolysis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SiCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>.","All of the above"],"answer":2,"solution":"<blockquote><p>Silicones are synthetic organo-silicon polymers containing repeated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>SiO</mi></math> units. Since the empirical formula is same as that of a ketone <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>CO</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, the name silicon has been given to these materials. They can be formed by hydrolysis of dichlorosilanes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SiCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SiCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>⟶</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>Si</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>HCl</mi></math></p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252Fc7856c4e-79e0-4292-850d-d703c5b70342-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fc7856c4e-79e0-4292-850d-d703c5b70342-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fc7856c4e-79e0-4292-850d-d703c5b70342-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 505px; height: 239px;\" /></p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2018","_marksId":"64c0e7fb6f5bfc7ab78ae989"},{"id":17700,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Ordinary glass is:","options":["Sodium silicate","Calcium silicate","Sodium and calcium silicate","Mixed salt of $\\mathrm{Na}$ and $\\mathrm{Ca}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Normal glass is calcium alkali silicate glass made by fusing the alkali metal carbonate, $\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2015","_marksId":"64c0e7cd6f5bfc7ab78ae805"},{"id":17701,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Which of the following is the most basic oxide?","options":["$\\mathrm{Sb}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{Bi}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{SeO}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"More the oxidation state of the central atom (metal) more is its acidity. Hence $\\mathrm{SeO}_{2}(\\mathrm{O} . \\mathrm{S} .$ of $\\mathrm{Se}=+4)$ is acidic. Further for a given O.S., the basic character of the oxides increases with the increasing size of the central atom. Thus $\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{Sb}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ are amphoteric and $\\mathrm{Bi}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is basic.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2015","_marksId":"64c0e7ce6f5bfc7ab78ae807"},{"id":17702,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Graphite is a","options":["molecular solid","covalent solid","ionic solid","metallic solid"],"answer":1,"solution":"Graphite is covalent solid.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2015","_marksId":"64c0e7cf6f5bfc7ab78ae815"},{"id":17703,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Among $\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}, \\mathrm{SiO}_{2}, \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$, the correct\norder of acid strength is:","options":["$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\lt \\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\\lt \\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\lt \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\\lt \\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\lt \\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\lt \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\lt \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\lt \\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\\lt \\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\lt \\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\\lt \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\lt \\mathrm{SO}_{2}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252FebJ1lXX8iQocLFz2YT5-cWGQdpuJ-avN5Hg4fLWAbO0.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FebJ1lXX8iQocLFz2YT5-cWGQdpuJ-avN5Hg4fLWAbO0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FebJ1lXX8iQocLFz2YT5-cWGQdpuJ-avN5Hg4fLWAbO0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2011","_marksId":"64c0e7926f5bfc7ab78ae603"},{"id":17704,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Which of the following is similar to graphite","options":["$\\underline{B}$","$\\mathrm{BN}$","$\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}$","$\\mathrm{B}_{4} \\mathrm{C}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Graphite and boron nitride have similar structure.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2010","_marksId":"64c0e7856f5bfc7ab78ae58e"},{"id":17705,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 13 & 14)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Three separate samples of a solution of a single salt gave these results. One formed a white precipitate with excess ammonia solution, one formed a white precipitate with dil. HCl solution and one formed a black precipitate with $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$. The salt could be","options":["$\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{Pb}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}$","$\\mathrm{Hg}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}$","$\\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Pb}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\rightarrow \\mathrm{Pb}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2} \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}(\\text { white ppt }) \\\\\n\\mathrm{Pb}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{PbCl}_{2} \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3}(\\text { whiteppt }) \\\\\n\\mathrm{Pb}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{PbS} \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3} \\text { (black) }\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2010","_marksId":"64c0e7886f5bfc7ab78ae5a8"}]}